# Reviewed historical hits for `node scripts/check-public-safety.mjs --history`.
#
# History is immutable, so a hit that was committed before the gate had a rule
# (or before a line carried the opt-out marker) cannot be fixed in place. Each
# entry is an exact fingerprint `commit:path:rule:sha256` where sha256 is the
# SHA-256 (hex) of the flagged value, and allows ONE value in ONE file of ONE
# commit; nothing else. To print the fingerprint of each waivable finding, run
# `node scripts/check-public-safety.mjs --history --fingerprints` locally (it is
# refused in CI, whose public log never shows values or hashes).
#
# Rules (enforced by the gate, which also scans this file like any other):
# - Never write a flagged value here, not even in a comment: describe it.
# - Every entry needs a justification comment directly above it (or above its
#   block), written by someone who looked at the content.
# - Only SYNTHETIC values may be listed. A real address, hostname, e-mail, key
#   or env file needs history rewriting and rotation instead. Entries for the
#   rules forbidden-file, private-key-block, email, railway-public-host and
#   railway-private-host are refused outright.
# - Entries that no longer match anything are reported as warnings (e.g. after
#   a history rewrite).
#
# Reviewed 3 Nov 2026 (CHR-66 follow-up F8, migrated to hashed values for N4).
# Every value below is a synthetic parser input, a documentation comment or a
# rule description; none is a real address, hostname or identifier, and none
# was ever contacted.

